         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "08b8faee-c9d5-4195-af8d-43573971f07d",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "inlove",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "816d0d03-bd90-4bcd-84ef-a2316dc68eff",
               "text": "Hitchcock, Don",
               "page": "Homo erectus - Homo ergaster",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2013",
                  "month": "-1",
                  "day": "-1"
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "ab1dda1e-95c2-4087-8299-e851e58bdc6f",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=tree&arg=33c83383-b9b5-43ec-987d-68f027f342b2",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Human Species\n\nThe time of existence of human species is a period that ranges from thousands of years up to a few million years.\n\nThis time was determined by different excavations that found skeletons that survived over time, such as bones of *Homo ergaster*, a species that lived almost 2 million years ago.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown/html/text",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<img src=\"https://www.historytracers.org/images/DonsMaps/1594.jpg\" />",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "*Image made by Don Hitchcock and available on Don's Maps.*",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "504c51fb-461d-4b92-99fa-6e0ba81d5c5e",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "504c51fb-461d-4b92-99fa-6e0ba81d5c5e",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "inlove",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "a1a7c478-9061-4160-ba43-301c081733e9",
               "text": "ESA",
               "page": "https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Planck/History_of_cosmic_structure_formation",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "16e68adc-2955-43d3-9413-5421557a40b7",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=atlas&atlas_page=a6ee2962-6912-4035-ae74-fa6bda6e1cc3",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Life and the Universe\n\nWhen we talk about all life, about the creation of planets and stars, the numbers used to talk about time become much larger, according to our measurements of the universe.\n\nOur current measurements show that the universe is more than 13 billion years old, and that planet Earth is less than 5 billion years old.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<img src=\"https://www.historytracers.org/images/ESA/Planck_history_of_Universe.jpg\" />",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "*Evolution of the universe, according to the European Space Agency (ESA).*",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
"next": "5adb96b5-732c-43eb-b566-811d9e56e253",
          "prev": "",
          "jumpTo": "",
          "answer": null,
          "score": 1,
          "position": ""
       },
       {
          "id": "5adb96b5-732c-43eb-b566-811d9e56e253",
          "audio": "",
          "smile": "shocking",
          "source_menu": [
             {
                "type": 1,
                "uuid": "49878bec-a9fb-40b3-9421-cc86648e7a45",
                "text": "ESA/Gaia/DPAC, S. Payne-Wardenaar, L. McCallum et al",
                "page": "https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Gaia",
                "date_time": {
                   "type": "gregory",
                   "year": "2025",
                   "month": "",
                   "day": ""
                }
             },
             {
                "type": 0,
                "uuid": "ad06d072-85a8-4a96-a31b-88a1e08cafd6",
                "text": "ESA. How many stars are there in the Universe?",
                "page": "https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/How_many_stars_are_there_in_the_Universe",
                "date_time": {
                   "type": "gregory",
                   "year": "",
                   "month": "",
                   "day": ""
                }
             },
             {
                "type": 1,
                "uuid": "46d1c955-320f-4c04-8d9e-3bcb29436582",
                "text": "Original Text",
                "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=46d1c955-320f-4c04-8d9e-3bcb29436582",
                "date_time": {
                   "type": "gregory",
                   "year": "",
                   "month": "",
                   "day": ""
                }
             }
          ],
          "text": [
             {
                "text": "#### The Size of the Universe\n\nIt is not only the age of the universe that is gigantic. When we look at its dimensions, we also see other staggering quantities.\n\nThe first step is to look at our cosmic neighborhood. We live in a vast region called the Milky Way. In it alone, we have about 100 billion stars - that is, 100,000,000,000. If you counted one star per second, non-stop, it would take you over 3,000 years to finish.",
                "source": [],
                "date_time": null,
                "isTable": false,
                "imgdesc": "",
                "format": "markdown",
                "PostMention": ""
             },
             {
                "text": "<img src=\"https://www.historytracers.org/images/ESA/Zoom_into_Gaia_s_star-formation_map_highlighted.jpg\" />",
                "source": [],
                "date_time": [],
                "isTable": false,
                "imgdesc": "",
                "format": "html",
                "PostMention": ""
             },
             {
                "text": "*Star-forming regions in the Milky Way. ESA/Gaia/DPAC.*",
                "source": [],
                "date_time": [],
                "isTable": false,
                "imgdesc": "",
                "format": "markdown",
                "PostMention": ""
             }
          ],
          "desc": null,
          "next": "5f9e8847-d1bc-4862-a66d-a06c5af9aead",
